# What each profile's own SUnit suites score, as of the last time somebody
# looked.  Read by tests/run_profiles.sh, which `make test` runs.
#
# This is a RATCHET, not a target.  The rule is that neither number may fall:
# a suite that ran 633 tests must still run 633, and a suite that passed 633
# must still pass 633.  Both directions matter, and the first one matters
# more than it looks -- a package whose tests stop being FOUND reports a
# perfect score, and that is exactly the shape of the failure this file
# exists to catch.  The Chronology suites spent a while reporting nothing at
# all because four classes deadlocked the runner, and nothing noticed.
#
# Improving a number fails too, with a message saying so.  That is
# deliberate: an unrecorded improvement is an improvement nothing is
# protecting, and the next regression silently spends it.  Edit the line in
# the same commit that earns it.
#
# Four profiles are at 100%; pharo-weak is one short, on purpose, and the row
# below records both numbers so that the shortfall cannot grow.  Two profiles
# reach their score with something excluded and each says so in its own text:
# pharo-announcements excludes WeakAnnouncerTest, and pharo-weak excludes a
# handful of classes it has no provider for.  pharo-weak no longer excludes the
# four Dictionary tests -- see the tail of this file.
#
# pharo-weak was 12/32 until the profile was given the classes its subjects are
# written against -- Pharo's WeakSet is a Pharo Set subclass and this profile
# was running it on 1983's -- and until the collector stopped marking a
# context's stack above its stack pointer.
#
# pharo-collections runs Pharo's Set, Dictionary, IdentityDictionary, Bag and
# MethodDictionary against the package's OWN tests, 469 of them, and all 469
# pass.  It was 115 when they were first wired in, and the grind from there
# found faults in this system rather than in the tests: a block with a
# trailing period answered nil, Object>>copy did not send postCopy so every
# copy of a Pharo collection shared its storage, becomeForward: did not
# exist, and the collector refused ephemerons.  Two of those four were
# compiler and object-memory bugs that had nothing to do with collections.
#
# A profile with no SUnit at all (bluebook) is not listed: it has no
# TestCase, so there is nothing to ask it.
#
# lib/Library-Tests adds 45 tests of the 1983 library itself -- the numeric
# tower, the collections, strings and streams -- so every profile that
# requires st2026 gains them too.  They found three bugs on their first run:
# a negative large-integer LITERAL lost its sign, a literal wider than 64
# bits wrapped, and the parser dropped whatever followed a complete method
# body instead of refusing it.
#
# lib/Database-Tests adds 43 more, and every number below moved by exactly 43
# for the same reason: they all require st2026.  A row that moved by anything
# else would be the interesting case, and there isn't one.
#
# Those 43 are the half of the database work that needs no database -- the
# join-path search, the SQL the query builder generates, and the exact
# decimals.  They found two real faults on their first run.  The scanner that
# reads table names out of an expression skipped every parenthesised group as
# a subquery, so COUNT(employee.employee_id) hid the table being counted, and
# the query joined nothing and answered a count over a cross product -- SQL
# that runs, returns numbers, and is wrong.  And a join to an ALIASED table
# wrote the real table name in its ON clause, referring to a table the FROM
# clause never introduced.
#
# lib/Json-Tests adds 101, and every number below moved by exactly 101 for the
# same reason the database's 43 did: they all require st2026.  Something over
# a third of the 99 are documents the parser must REFUSE -- a trailing comma,
# an unquoted name, a bare word, a leading zero, a lone surrogate, a second
# document after the first.  Those are the ones with anything to catch.  This
# package is deliberately stricter than the org.json that Kiss carries, and
# strictness is invisible to every test of a valid document: a reader that
# quietly accepted all six would pass the rest of the suite.
#
# The half that DOES need a database is not counted here and must not be:
# lib/Database-Live-Tests needs an ODBC driver manager and a driver, which a
# machine building this system cannot be assumed to have.  A suite that
# cannot run where it is checked would report a perfect score every time,
# which is the exact shape of failure this file exists to catch.  It has a
# profile of its own -- profiles/database-live.profile, 234 tests through
# SQLite -- and is run deliberately.  234 rather than the 129 that used to be
# written here for the same reason every row above moved: the profile requires
# st2026 and so inherits its suites, and lib/Json-Tests added 99 of them.
#
# lib/Library-Tests gained two more, for OrderedCollection>>removeIndex:,
# which now takes the index at: takes and answers the element -- 1983's took
# an index into the Array behind the collection, and `removeIndex: 2' on
# 1, 2, 3 left 2 and 3.  Every row moved by two; database-live is 230.
#
# DelayTest adds four: Delay's timing process was rewritten so that a stale
# timer signal is a look at the clock rather than the end of the process,
# and one of the four sends that stale signal on purpose.  Every row moved
# by four; database-live is 234.
#
# lib/Network-Tests adds twenty-eight, over the loopback interface on a port
# the system picks, so they need no network and no fixed port and run on any
# build machine.  Each makes its own listener and closes both ends in an
# ensure:.  The ones with anything to catch are the ones that wait: an accept
# before anybody connects, a line split across two sends, a 300,000-byte
# transfer through a producer on another process, a close from one process
# that must wake a waiter on another, and a wait that a full collection runs
# through.  Every row moved by twenty-eight; database-live is 262.
#
# lib/Tonel-Tests adds nineteen: the reader on every construct that can hide a
# bracket, the writer's round trip character for character, and the registry
# against a real file -- loaded, reloaded when the file changes, and written
# back through the same three messages the Browser sends.  Two of them found
# faults older than the package: the image's own compiler did not read `:='
# and hung a headless run on the notifier it opened to say so, and a
# bootstrapped image logged every compiled method's source over the last one's.
# Every row moved by nineteen; database-live is 281.
#
# lib/JSON-RPC-Server-Tests adds twenty-nine: the request parser on text --
# more than a third of them requests it must refuse -- the response writer,
# and a server on the loopback interface driven by a client written on the raw
# socket, so that what is tested is the wire.  One of them, an idle connection
# closed after the keep-alive time, found that on Linux a close() from one
# thread does not release a descriptor another thread is blocked in poll() on,
# and the network layer now wakes its thread on every close.  Every row moved
# by twenty-nine; database-live is 310.
#
# lib/Rest-Server-Tests adds sixteen, driving the whole stack -- Kiss's wire
# format, the three framework methods, sessions, a service loaded from a Tonel
# file and reloaded when the file changes, uploads, a binary reply, the error
# codes, sixteen calls at once -- over the loopback interface against the
# back end in tests/rest-backend.  No database here; the half that needs one
# is lib/Rest-Server-Live-Tests, in database-live with the rest of that kind.
# Every row moved by sixteen; database-live is 331 with its own three.
#
# lib/Crypto-Tests adds twenty-nine: SHA-256, HMAC-SHA256 and PBKDF2 through
# primitive 209 against their published answers (the arithmetic itself is
# checked in C, in tests/unit/test_crypto.c); Base64 both ways, both
# paddings, and what it refuses; and the stored password format against the
# hash Kiss's own schema.sqlite holds for its demo user -- the one test that
# runs six hundred thousand rounds, and the proof that a users table Kiss
# made logs in here unchanged.  Every row moved by twenty-nine; database-live
# is 360.
#
# lib/Rest-Server-Tests adds two for the server's startup hook: an Init class
# in the back end is told init: before the listener opens and init2: with nil
# when there is no database, and a back end without one is told nothing; and
# an Init that raises leaves the server not listening.  The half that needs a
# database -- init2: committed, or rolled back when it raises -- is two more
# in lib/Rest-Server-Live-Tests against tests/rest-init-backend.  Every row
# moved by two; database-live is 364.
#
# lib/Web-Demo-Tests adds nine: the demo application -- demo/backend, and
# Kiss's own front end copied under demo/frontend -- started on a port the
# system picks and driven as a browser drives it: the pages by the paths the
# browser asks for, the three addNumbers by the front end's three names, an
# upload, and what the database screens say when there is no database.  The
# half with a database -- Kiss's user logging in with the hash Kiss stored,
# the phone list and the users screen through a session -- is eight more in
# lib/Web-Demo-Live-Tests, in database-live with the rest of that kind.  Every
# row moved by nine; database-live is 381.
#
# lib/Tonel-Tests adds two, for a fault the demo found on the server that no
# test had: a method sending a selector defined later in its own file loaded
# in every test and failed under `st80 -serve', because 1983's Parser puts a
# new selector to the editor's user and TonelReader had no `editor' -- so
# the outcome was whatever an unhandled doesNotUnderstand: answered, nil in
# the bootstrap process and not nil in a loaded image.  A new selector is
# now interned as written for a requestor with no user, and an undeclared
# variable is still refused by name.  Every row moved by two; database-live
# is 383.
#
# lib/JSON-RPC-Server-Tests adds six for another origin -- a page on this
# host at another port, which is Kiss's development layout: a preflight
# answered with the permissions and no handler run, an origin on this host
# echoed back, another host served but given no permission and its preflight
# refused, no Origin served as before, an OPTIONS without one reaching the
# handler; and the host comparison on text.  Every row moved by six;
# database-live is 389.
#
# lib/HTTP-Client-Tests adds thirteen and lib/LLM-Tests five, with one more in
# lib/Web-Demo-Tests: an HTTP client against HttpServer on the loopback
# interface and against a listener of the test's own answering what
# HttpServer never sends -- a chunked body, a body that ends with the
# connection, a 204, a reply that is not HTTP -- plus the URL parser on
# text; Ollama's three questions answered by that listener the way the real
# server answers them, and Kiss's four text substitutions; and the demo's
# OllamaQuery asked whether an Ollama is up.  And one more in
# lib/Network-Tests, for what that service found on a machine with an Ollama
# running: `localhost' resolved to ::1 before 127.0.0.1, the server listened
# on 127.0.0.1 alone, and connectTo:port: took the first address only, so an
# Ollama that was up was `connection refused'.  It tries every address now,
# and the test listens on 127.0.0.1 alone and connects by name.  Every row
# moved by twenty; database-live is 409.
#
# lib/Clipboard-Tests adds four, for the bridge between the 1983 editor's
# clipboard and the system's: with no window -- which is every test run --
# there is nothing there and the editor keeps its own buffer; the line ends
# are translated both ways; a wrong argument to the primitive is nil and not
# an error.  Every row moved by four; database-live is 413.
#
# lib/Web-Demo-Tests adds one, and loses none, when the demo's three
# addNumbers services became one: Kiss's REST Services screen offers a
# Groovy, a Java and a Lisp back end, a choice that means nothing where every
# service is Smalltalk, so the screen has one button and the back end one
# file, MyService.  The test that called all three names now calls the one;
# the new test reads the screen itself back through the server and refuses
# the three buttons, because the front end is a copy of Kiss's and a re-copy
# would bring them back against a back end that no longer has them.  Every
# row moved by one; database-live is 414.
#
# lib/Rest-Server-Tests adds one for what a browser found the same day, after
# every test, the manual's curl and the smoke runs had passed: Kiss's front
# end spells a service `services/Adder', with a slash, and the loader took
# `services.Adder' alone -- so in a browser every screen but the login was
# `not a class name'.  The new test sends both spellings and gets one class;
# and the demo's suites, here and in database-live, now spell every service
# as the front end does, since a suite that spells it its own way passes
# whatever the browser gets.  Every row moved by one; database-live is 415.
#
# lib/LLM-Tests adds twenty-nine and lib/HTTP-Client-Tests and
# lib/Network-Tests two each, for the language models and the TLS under
# them.  The LLM tests run against a listener of the test's own that keeps
# every request, so what is checked is the wire: the header each service
# wants, the body a request carries, a streamed reply cut into pieces
# across a chunk boundary, a tool call and its answer going back, an
# embedding, and what a refusal, a rate limit and an error mid-stream
# become.  No service is reached; profiles/llm-live.profile reaches them
# with keys, and profiles/internet-live.profile reaches real certificates
# -- neither is here, for the reason database-live is not.  The four
# others: a reply read by the line as it arrives, a body left unread until
# asked for, an https URL to a peer speaking plain text refused with
# OpenSSL's reason, and the environment read.  Every row moved by
# thirty-three; database-live is 448.
#
# lib/Library-Tests adds ten, for the paths a person types into the File
# List's top pane: a leading tilde made into the home directory, `.' and
# `..' and a bare directory name entered rather than matched, and the dot
# segments taken back out so the pane keeps saying a name that can be typed
# in again.  Three of them are for faults that pane already had: `..' went
# through 1983's trailing-period rule and became `.', a file name nothing
# answered to; a pattern was matched against the whole designator, so
# `Files-F*' in lib was compared against lib/Files-Fixes and found nothing;
# and a directory that could not be walked answered ITSELF rather than no
# names, so a mistyped path opened a doesNotUnderstand on collect:.
# Every row moved by ten; database-live is 458.
#
# Two more for `new' on the File List's list menu: where a bare name typed
# into it is taken -- the directory the list is showing, which the pattern
# is the only record of -- and the places that have no directory to take it
# in.  Every row moved by two; database-live is 460.
#
# One more for the list pane keeping its selection when its rows are rebuilt.
# The pane is opened with no selector to ask the model which row to
# highlight, so it answered `nothing' and reported `fileName: nil' back, and
# every announcement of #fileName threw the selection away -- a renamed file
# came back unselected, and a new file had nothing behind the text pane.
# Every row moved by one; database-live is 461.
#
# Two more for the mark a directory carries in the list pane.  What a
# directory holds is files and directories, and a row that is only a name
# does not say which of the two it is -- so a directory is written with
# ` (dir)' after it, and `get contents' stops being a guess.  The second test
# is for the way a row is turned back into a name: looked up in the list
# rather than cut back, because ` (dir)' is a legal ending for a POSIX file
# name and a file that really has one must stay selectable.  Every row moved
# by two; database-live is 463.
#
# Two more for the way up.  A directory listing holds `..', so going up is
# the same gesture as going anywhere else rather than a path that has to be
# typed; the row says `..' and the name behind it says it after the directory
# the list is showing, which keeps it a designator that opens.  The second
# test is for the places that have no way up: the root, where `/..' is `/', a
# directory that is not there, a bare file name, and a list of patterns that
# is showing no one place.  Every row moved by two; database-live is 465.
#
# Seventy-seven more with the Bugs3 fixes, a third outside-in audit that
# reached every layer at once.  lib/Concurrency-Tests gains MonitorTest (4)
# and ProcessTest (11) for a Monitor that is reentrant and for terminate,
# suspend and signalException: reaching a process another worker is running,
# and DelayTest three more for a Delay validated before it is published.
# lib/Library-Tests gains St80ExceptionTest (2) for an ensure: block that
# ran twice and a handler guard that is not a class, and eighteen across
# St80CollectionTest, St80NumberTest and St80TextTest for copies that copy,
# a SortedCollection that keeps its block, streams over an
# OrderedCollection, Time reading noon as noon, a Random with a seed and a
# stable sort.  lib/JSON-RPC-Server-Tests, lib/Json-Tests,
# lib/HTTP-Client-Tests and lib/Rest-Server-Tests gain twenty-one for what a
# hostile client sends: lowercase percent-escapes, a JSON number of three
# thousand digits, a header value with a line break in it, the RFC 7230
# framing refusals, a symlink out of the doc root, a chunk size of
# ffffffffffffffff.  lib/Tonel-Tests gains eighteen for the files that come
# back as they went out: an extension file written back as an extension, a
# weak class and a trait composition round-tripping, a file with a bad
# method loading nothing, a BOM.  Every row moved by seventy-seven;
# database-live is 542.
#
# Bugs4, a fourth audit, moved every row again and by different amounts, which
# is the first time that has happened.  Every row gains the forty-one tests
# that go with st2026 -- twenty-three for what a hostile client sends and what
# a server answers back (an accept loop that survives a failed accept, a
# descriptor closed after every request, a form field whose digits are counted
# before it is turned into a number, CR and LF refused in a client's headers, a
# short body detected, a 1xx skipped, ambiguous framing refused), fourteen for
# the numeric tower and the collections (floorLog: of an infinity, a
# SortedCollection filled in ascending order, a Fraction that reduces, sorted
# on an OrderedCollection), and four for a Delay across the millisecond clock's
# wrap.  database-live, which is named only in this comment because it needs
# an ODBC driver a build machine cannot be assumed to have, is 584.
#
# And pharo-weak gains five hundred and twelve more, because the four
# Dictionary test classes it used to exclude are now in it, and their package
# brings DictionaryTest's whole root with it.  That exclusion had been hiding
# the one thing the profile exists for: WeakKeyDictionary and
# WeakIdentityKeyDictionary were not weak at all -- every key was held strongly
# and never reclaimed -- and the profile passed 524 of 524 without noticing,
# because the only tests that would have said so were the excluded ones.  They
# are weak now: ephemerons are allocated as ephemerons whatever built them, a
# Finalizer process drains what the collector has fired and sends each one
# #mourn, and a copy of a weak collection is still weak.
#
# ONE test is failing and is recorded failing, which this file has not had to
# say before.  WeakKeyDictionaryTest>>testClearing collects, asserts on the
# next line that the dictionary is still full -- "keys are gone but not yet
# finalized" -- and on the line after that that it is empty.  Both hold only if
# #mourn reaches a thousand associations in the gap between two consecutive
# sends.  On Cog it does, because the interrupt check falls on a method return.
# Here the process switch is looked at once per bytecode, so the finalizer has
# the processor before the first of those two sends.  It is a statement about
# where a preemption lands, not about weak keys, and the profile's own text
# says so at length.  Recording 1077 and 1076 keeps the ratchet doing its job
# in both directions: a second failure makes the passed number fall.
#
# One more in lib/Network-Tests, for the last of the Bugs4 hangs to be
# found and the only one this file could not have caught.  A timed socket
# wait forks a watchdog to end it, and a wait that readiness won left that
# watchdog waiting out its whole timeout with the VM's timer armed for it.
# The scheduler will not say `every process is blocked' while anything is
# pending, so one abandoned watchdog held the deadlock verdict shut for as
# long as its timeout -- the language-model client asks for three hundred
# seconds and the runner gives up after three hundred, so a run that had
# genuinely stopped reported NOTHING, which is the failure this file is
# for and the one shape of it this file cannot see.  Every row moved by
# one; database-live is 585.
#
# profile               run  passed
st2026                  546  546
pharo-announcements     577  577
pharo-time              1167 1167
pharo-weak              1078 1077
pharo-collections       1003 1003
